{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_4","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"rg -n 'derive-verified|signed-entries|include-handoff|siteset/stage' src test collab/yang-php\"","aggregated_output":"collab/yang-php/1794570178000-engineer-p7b-ticks-j.prompt.md:6:# Task: P7-b part J — WIRE the site-set consumer chain into the PHP linker + entry paths. SOLE acceptance gate: include-handoff-test green.\ncollab/yang-php/1794570178000-engineer-p7b-ticks-j.prompt.md:10:signed-entry/entry-datoms/discover/signed-entries + derive-verified (manifest-bound verifier,\ncollab/yang-php/1794570178000-engineer-p7b-ticks-j.prompt.md:12:served). Nothing wired yet. Pattern reference: yang.python.antlr.siteset/stage + interpreter\ncollab/yang-php/1794570178000-engineer-p7b-ticks-j.prompt.md:16:1. PHP linker stage: discovery from signed linker index datoms -> derive-verified at link time\ncollab/yang-php/1794570178000-engineer-p7b-ticks-j.prompt.md:25:include-handoff-test GREEN (semantic/stack/register `LT`, 600s budget); tick-fixtures stays\ncollab/yang-php/1794569650000-engineer-p7b-ticks-h.gpt-6.1-terra.stdout.log:5:{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_1\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc 'cat docs/design/datom.world.md && cat collab/yang-php/1794569546000*findings.md'\",\"aggregated_output\":\"---\\ndescription: Core philosophy, foundational axioms, invariants, primitives (tuples, streams, host boundaries), architectural commitments, design principles, code style, and development workflow for datom.world\\n---\\n\\n# DATOM.WORLD ARCHITECTURAL FOUNDATIONS & DEVELOPMENT RULES\\n\\n## Core Philosophy\\n\\nEverything is data.\\nInterpretation > abstraction.\\nExplicit causality > implicit assumptions.\\nRestrictions are a feature.\\n\\n### Foundational Axioms\\n\\n1. **Everything is a Stream**: Portable I/O and system data flow through append-only streams; hosts may also install explicit local functions that perform direct I/O.\\n2. **Interpretation Creates Semantics**: Data is syntax; semantics only emerge through interpretation (one truth, many perspectives).\\n   At system scale, a \\\"system\\\" is an emergent property of interpreters of\\n   pure data, not an object, service, or stored artifact. Find the\\n   interpreters and the data they share to find the system.\\n3. **Code and State are Datoms**: Code (Universal AST) and runtime execution state are structured datoms, enabling syntax independence and structural reflection.\\n4. **Everything is a Continuation**: Computation is modeled as serializable, portable continuations that can pause, travel across streams, and resume anywhere.\\n\\n## Non-Negotiable Invariants\\n\\n- Do not introduce hidden global state.\\n- Do not introduce implicit control flow.\\n- Do not introduce callbacks; every callback is an event on a stream.\\n- Do not introduce shared mutable state.\\n- Do not collapse interpretation and execution into the same layer.\\n- Do not assume graphs: graphs must be constructed explicitly from tuples.\\n- Do not lose information (unitarity): streams are append-only and no datom is destroyed, only reinterpreted. The Universal AST is never lost: it is always retrievable wherever code is lowered, by address or by a query over its datoms. Every derived form (semantic vector, native images, indexes) is an interpretation of it and may drop facts only because the AST remains.\\n\\n## Tuples and Datoms\\n\\nTuples are elements in an open moduli space, graded by dimension $n$. A tuple can be any dimension/size.\\nA datom is the canonical persistent tuple `[e a v t m]` (entity, attribute, value, transaction, metadata). Persistent facts are immutable datoms.\\nSource scope belongs to the interpreter, never to an appended tuple slot. Queries keep physical sources as separate db-values and express union or joins explicitly.\\n\\nSee [`docs/design/datom.md`](./datom.md) for full specification.\\n\\n## Streams\\n\\n`dao.stream` is an I/O monad and the system's portable I/O ABI. Functions consume streams and produce streams.\\nHosts may install arbitrary local functions, including direct I/O; their availability and migration semantics must be explicit.\\nPortable behavior cannot assume them; portable side effects appear as stream emissions. Direct-I/O host modules are explicitly local and non-migratable (owner decision, 2026-10-23).\\nStreams are values that can be sent through streams.\\nStreams carry whatever values the consumer needs: no datom requirement, no privileged payload shape.\\n\\nA stream has no privileged reader, and its meaning is not in it. Any number\\nof interpreters may observe the same medium, and each constructs its own\\nsemantics from the same batches: a `yin.vm` evaluator observing a program\\nstream constructs CESK state and executes it; `dao.space.index` observing\\nthe very same stream materializes covered indexes, so `dao.space.query/q`\\nanswers Datalog over the program as it grows. Neither knows the other\\nexists. The medium is their only coupling, and each observer's ignorance of\\nthe other is load-bearing: the evaluator never consults an index to run,\\nand the index never evaluates a form to index it. This is how axiom 2\\nbecomes architecture: one stream, many interpretations, none of them\\nthe stream's own.\\n\\nFunctions can take anything and return anything.\\nAgents are functions, closures, or continuations in `yin.vm` that communicate portably with the outside world through `dao.stream`. Portable agent behavior is specified through stream effects; host-local functions may also perform direct I/O.\\n\\nSee [`docs/design/dao.stream.md`](./dao.stream.md) for full stream specification.\\nSee [`docs/design/yin.vm.streams-all-the-way-down.md`](./yin.vm.streams-all-the-way-down.md) for VM architecture.\\n\\n### Host Boundaries\\n\\nA host boundary is the dao.stream abstraction boundary; implementations do not add contracts.\\n[datom.world.abi.md](./datom.world.abi.md) owns the doctrine, owner rulings, taxonomy, floor and custody rules.\\n\\n## Architectural Commitments\\n\\n- **`dao.jing` is syntax, agents are semantics**: the content-addressed encoding stays passive and payload-agnostic.\\n- **Macros are stream topology**: an evaluator knows nothing about macro expansion; expansion happens on the syntax side of a medium boundary, upstream of any evaluator.\\n- **Peer observers, one stream**: a generic evaluator and `dao.space` independently observe the same stream topic, as described under Streams. This is not a claim about any two consumers regardless of what they consume.\\n- **Observers are independent and optional**: nothing requires a medium to have any particular set of consumers. Which observers exist for a stream is a per-composition choice, not a design-time commitment.\\n\\n## Design Principles\\n\\nCode quality is measured by malleability: how cheaply can changes adapt without breaking promises?\\nStigmergic coordination emerges from sharing data across streams.\\n\\n**Systems emerge from interpreters of pure data.** Streams are the\\ncomposition medium; each interpreter gives the same data a perspective\\nwithout collapsing interpretation into execution or owning the truth.\\n\\n- `dao.space` emerges from `dao.space.index` and `dao.space.query` over\\n  ordinary streams.\\n- ShiBi capability, metering, and budget emerge from different\\n  interpreters over the same tuples; no token object is stored.\\n- The four VMs interpret one Universal AST; many syntaxes feed that AST.\\n- The semantic AST in `dao.space` is queryable facts projected by the\\n  auto-index observer.\\n- Client and server are roles in one stigmergic behavior implemented by\\n  interpreters, not distinct kinds of nodes.\\n\\n**Derive, don't persist.** Before adding a tag, slot, or field to any\\ncanonical content-hashed structure (an AST grammar, a datom schema, a row\\nshape), check whether a correctly scoped Datalog query over\\n`dao.space.query/q` can already compute the fact. If it can, it belongs as a\\nquery or projection: new persisted structure migrates every downstream\\nconsumer and duplicates authority over a fact a query derives fresh. The\\nretired `:global` tag in\\n[`docs/design/yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md`](./yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md) is the\\ncase study, including the scoping bug that a naive query introduced.\\n\\nSee [`docs/agents/malleability.md`](../agents/malleability.md) for complete design guidance (cohesion, coupling, bounded complexity, evolution, testing strategy).\\nSee [`docs/agents/vocabulary.md`](../agents/vocabulary.md) for domain vocabulary reference (biology, economics, physics, philosophy).\\n\\n## Code Style\\n\\n- Prefer simple data over rich types.\\n- Prefer total functions over partial ones.\\n- Prefer declarative pipelines over imperative logic.\\n- Avoid cleverness.\\n- Name things after what they do to streams.\\n\\n## Development Workflow\\n\\n- Do not invent abstractions that hide streams.\\n- Do not suggest mainstream frameworks unless explicitly asked.\\n- Do not optimize prematurely.\\n- When uncertain, ask at the architectural level, not the implementation level.\\n- Explanations should align with Plan 9 / Datomic / Lisp lineage.\\n- Program store writes go through `engine/store-put`; any other store write\\n  the store-write audit (`test/yin/vm/store_write_audit_test.clj`)\\n  detects must be on its allowlist; its docstring states the residual it\\n  leaves to review.\\n\\n### Problem Decomposition\\n\\nIf a problem appears complex:\\n1. Decompose it into streams.\\n2. Identify invariants.\\n3. Restrict degrees of freedom.\\n4. Make causality explicit.\\n5. Let structure emerge from constraints.\\n\\n## Operational & Specialized Guides\\n\\n- **Advanced Concepts**: See [`docs/agents/advanced-concepts.md`](../agents/advanced-concepts.md) for parallel transport, capability tokens, and entanglement.\\n- **Build & Test**: See [`docs/agents/build-n-test.md`](../agents/build-n-test.md) for build, lint, and test commands and TDD guidelines.\\n- **Engineering Team Roster**: See [`docs/agents/team.md`](../agents/team.md) for engineering team roles and LLM model assignments.\\n- **Agent Delegation**: See [`docs/agents/roles/orchestrator.md`](../agents/roles/orchestrator.md) for collaboration protocols and delegating tasks or reviews through `agy`, `cmd`, `glm`, `deepseek`, and `codex`; each orchestrator session has its own append-only log at `orchestrator-logs/<unix-time-stamp>-<brief-orchestrator-descriptive-name>.md` under the repository root.\\n- **Website & Content**: See [`docs/agents/format.md`](../agents/format.md) for `.chp` and `.blog` EDN/Hiccup file specifications.\\nCreated-GMT: 2026-11-13 11:32:26 GMT\\nCreated-Local: 2026-11-13 18:32:26 +0700\\nCoding-Agent: codex\\nRole: Engineer\\nStatus: partial; include gate red; P7-b part G incomplete\\n\\nPushed c347f086 and b784516b to p7b-ticks. PHP unit publication now writes a content-addressed occurrence site set and a record binding it to the manifest, canonical tree root, frontend revision and lowering profile. The set preserves the mark's explicit unit operand; empty sets are published. The verifier rejects missing or mismatched metadata and checks the set's canonical ordering. PHP SCC aliases publish their own empty sets; the bundle retains its marks. The shared publication seam defaults to the previous alias behavior for other frontends.\\n\\nSequential checks: siteset-test initially 1 test / 4 assertions green; include-handoff-test completed under the 600-second limit with 3 failures, no errors: semantic, stack and register each produced `L` instead of `LT`. It did not time out; no claim of a dao.jing-class stall. The expanded siteset-test passed 3 tests / 11 assertions. clj-kondo over the four changed code/test files reported 0 errors and 0 warnings. The commit hook formatted the staged files. No Docker or oracle run; existing fixture statuses remain pending.\\n\\nRemaining work: site publication and verification alone do not implement signed discovery, verified link-time derivation, hook dependency publication/pinning, or bottom-up derived dependency and SCC alias rebinding. Entry production execution also still needs selected-profile derivation. The include test is red until these are integrated; the fixture harness currently uses a trusted uninstrumented source. Statement selection, adjacent suppression, hoisted semicolon threshold, nested-zero and registry mutation fixtures and fixes were not completed. The full fixture set, additivity checks and cljstyle gate were not run. Do not interpret these commits as production ticks or parity acceptance.\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}\ncollab/yang-php/1794569650000-engineer-p7b-ticks-h.gpt-6.1-terra.stdout.log:17:{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_7\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"cat test/yang/php/antlr/ticks_test.clj test/yang/php/antlr/linked_harness.cljc; cat src/cljc/yang/php/antlr/publication.cljc; rg -n 'site-set|site.set|signed|alias' src/cljc/yang/*publication* src/cljc/yin/vm/linker/publish.cljc\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"(ns yang.php.antlr.ticks-test\\n  (:require\\n    [clojure.test :refer [deftest is testing]]\\n    [yang.php.antlr.cst :as cst]\\n    [yang.php.antlr.e2e-harness :as h]\\n    [yang.php.antlr.linked-harness :as lh]\\n    [yang.php.antlr.programs-p7b :as p7b]))\\n\\n\\n(deftest tick-fixtures-test\\n  (doseq [{:keys [id source out units]} p7b/fixtures\\n          :when (nil? units)]\\n    (testing (name id)\\n      (is (= out (:php/out (h/run-raw (str \\\"<?php \\\" p7b/prologue source))))))))\\n\\n\\n(deftest include-handoff-test\\n  (let [{:keys [source units out]} (last p7b/fixtures)\\n        packets (update-vals (assoc units \\\"main.php\\\" (str \\\"<?php \\\" p7b/prologue source))\\n                             cst/parse-source)\\n        result (lh/run-fixture packets \\\"main.php\\\" {})]\\n    (doseq [[vm value] (:values result)]\\n      (is (= out (h/latin1->text (:php/out value))) (name vm)))))\\n(ns yang.php.antlr.linked-harness\\n  \\\"The linked profile's composition for the PHP tests (P6, docs/design/\\n   yang.php.md section 11): the namespace that requires this is the\\n   publisher of `php`, so it publishes the module once per process into one\\n   in-memory content store, publishes a fixture's non-entry units beside it\\n   bottom-up, and serves them to the entry unit's run over a\\n   `yin.vm.linker.interpreter` composition under `:trusted` derivation.\\n\\n   A linked run builds the entry unit's task on one of the three vector VMs\\n   with a link pair, then runs the VM and serves the pair alternately until\\n   the VM stops blocking, as the REPL's drive does. The AST walker is not a\\n   backend here: its link of `php` is refused until L-b.\\\"\\n  (:require\\n    [dao.jing.mem :as mem]\\n    [yang.core.call :as call]\\n    [yang.php.antlr.lower :as lower]\\n    [yang.php.antlr.portable-harness :as portable]\\n    [yang.php.antlr.prelude :as prelude]\\n    [yang.php.antlr.publication :as publication]\\n    [yin.vm :as vm]\\n    [yin.vm.debruijn.register :as rvm]\\n    [yin.vm.debruijn.stack :as dvm]\\n    [yin.vm.linker.interpreter :as link]\\n    [yin.vm.linker.publish :as publish]\\n    [yin.vm.semantic :as semantic]\\n    [yin.vm.semantic.derive :as derive]\\n    [yin.vm.semantic.linearize :as sr-linearize]\\n    [yin.vm.test-utils :as tu]))\\n\\n\\n(defn registry\\n  \\\"The host registry a linked PHP composition registers: the `require`\\n   handlers, then `cell`, `data` and `integer` under the portable limits.\\\"\\n  []\\n  (portable/linked-registry))\\n\\n\\n(def published\\n  \\\"`yang.call` and `php` published once into their own content store.\\\"\\n  (delay\\n    (let [store (mem/create-content-mem)\\n          call-result (publish/publish-module! store (call/module-spec (registry)))\\n          result (publish/publish-module! store (prelude/module-spec (registry)\\n                                                                     (:address call-result)))]\\n      {:store store,\\n       :result result,\\n       :address (:address result),\\n       :call-address (:address call-result)})))\\n\\n\\n(defn publish-units!\\n  \\\"`units` (`[{:name path :packet p :strict-types b}]`) published into\\n   `php`'s own content store, where its pinned requirement already lives:\\n   `publication/publish-units!`'s answer.\\\"\\n  [units]\\n  (publication/publish-units! (:store @published) (registry) (:address @published) units))\\n\\n\\n(defn source\\n  \\\"The serving composition over the published store: `name-env` names `php`\\n   and each published module, derivation `:trusted`, the test being the\\n   publisher.\\\"\\n  [name-env]\\n  (link/composition {:content-store (:store @published),\\n                     :name-env (merge {'php (:address @published),\\n                                       'yang.call (:call-address @published)} name-env),\\n                     :derivation :trusted}))\\n\\n\\n(def backends\\n  \\\"Each vector backend's root task over a program AST and composition.\\\"\\n  {:semantic (fn [ast opts]\\n               (semantic/load-ast (semantic/create-vm opts)\\n                                  (vm/ast->datoms ast)\\n                                  vm/ast-contract)),\\n   :stack (fn [ast opts]\\n            (dvm/create-vm (:image (derive/stack-image (:vector (sr-linearize/project ast))))\\n                           (assoc opts :contract vm/stack-contract))),\\n   :register (fn [ast opts]\\n               (rvm/create-vm (derive/register-image (:vector (sr-linearize/project ast)))\\n                              (assoc opts :contract vm/register-contract)))})\\n\\n\\n(defn composition\\n  \\\"A task's composition over `modules` and the link `pair`.\\\"\\n  [modules pair]\\n  {:make-stream tu/make-stream,\\n   :capability-secret tu/secret,\\n   :secret-source (fn [origin] (str tu/secret \\\"/\\\" (name origin))),\\n   :primitives vm/primitives,\\n   :modules modules,\\n   :link-request (:requests pair),\\n   :link-response (:responses pair)})\\n\\n\\n(defn drive\\n  \\\"Run `task` and serve `pair` from `src` alternately until the task stops\\n   blocking, at most 80 rounds. Returns `[task pair]`.\\\"\\n  [task pair src]\\n  (loop [task (vm/run task)\\n         pair pair\\n         i 0]\\n    (if (and (vm/blocked? task) (< i 80))\\n      (let [pair (:pair (link/serve {:pair pair, :source src}))]\\n        (recur (vm/run task) pair (inc i)))\\n      [task pair])))\\n\\n\\n(defn run-linked\\n  \\\"The finished task of the linked program `ast` on `backend`, served from\\n   `src`. A refused link surfaces as the program's own diagnostics.\\\"\\n  [backend ast src]\\n  (let [pair (link/make-pair)\\n        task ((get backends backend) ast (composition (registry) pair))]\\n    (first (drive task pair src))))\\n\\n\\n(defn name-env\\n  \\\"The name environment naming every published module of `result`\\n   (`publish-units!`'s answer): module symbol to address.\\\"\\n  [result]\\n  (merge (into {} (map (fn [[path address]] [(lower/path-module-symbol path) address]))\\n               (:addresses result))\\n         (:bundles result)))\\n\\n\\n(defn run-fixture\\n  \\\"`{backend value}` for the multi-unit fixture `units` (`{path packet}`,\\n   the entry unit `main`, default `lower/entry-path`) on the three vector\\n   VMs: the non-entry units are published bottom-up, the entry unit\\n   lowered `:linked` and run. `value` is `run-main`'s map.\\\"\\n  ([units] (run-fixture units lower/entry-path {}))\\n  ([units main opts]\\n   (let [entry (get units main)\\n         others (cond-> (mapv (fn [[path packet]] {:name path, :packet packet})\\n                              (dissoc units main))\\n                  (:class-map opts) (conj (assoc (:class-map opts) :name lower/classmap-name)))\\n         result (publish-units! others)]\\n     (when-not (= :ok (:status result))\\n       (throw (ex-info \\\"fixture units failed to publish\\\" {:result result})))\\n     (let [{:keys [ast warnings]}\\n           (lower/lower-program entry\\n                                (cond-> (merge {:prelude :linked,\\n                                                :path main,\\n                                                :paths (when (:computed? opts) (keys (:addresses result)))}\\n                                               (dissoc opts :computed? :src :request))\\n                                  (:class-map opts) (assoc :class-map true)\\n                                  (some? (:request opts)) (assoc :request-node\\n                                                                 (portable/request-node (:request opts)))))\\n           src (or (:src opts) (source (name-env result)))]\\n       {:warnings warnings,\\n        :values (into {}\\n                      (map (fn [k] [k (vm/value (run-linked k ast src))]))\\n                      (keys backends))}))))\\n(ns yang.php.antlr.publication\\n  \\\"P6 (docs/design/yang.php.md section 11): a set of compiled PHP units\\n   published as linker modules. The ordering, SCC bundling and publish loop\\n   are `yang.core.publication`'s; this namespace supplies the PHP hooks: the\\n   includes `lower/imported-unit-names` folds, the `lower/module-spec` and\\n   `lower/scc-module-specs` emitters and site sets; no binder table.\\n\\n   A unit is `{:name path :packet p :strict-types b}`, `path` its normalized\\n   relative path; the optional class map entry is `{:name \\\\\\\"$classmap\\\\\\\"\\n   :classes {class path} :paths [path ...]}`. Name assertions stay the\\n   linker's: this namespace publishes content, it signs nothing.\\\"\\n  (:require\\n    [yang.core.publication :as core]\\n    [yang.php.antlr.lower :as lower]\\n    [yang.php.antlr.siteset :as siteset]\\n    [yin.vm.linker.publish :as publish]))\\n\\n\\n(defn- class-map?\\n  \\\"The autoload class map entry of a unit set, `{:name \\\\\\\"$classmap\\\\\\\" :classes\\n   {class path} :paths [path ...]}`: it imports every path it lists, so it\\n   publishes last and an unpublished path is refused by name (plan P6 R9).\\\"\\n  [unit]\\n  (= lower/classmap-name (:name unit)))\\n\\n\\n(defn- config\\n  [publish-fn]\\n  {:imported-names (fn [{:keys [name packet], :as unit}]\\n                     (if (class-map? unit)\\n                       (lower/classmap-paths unit)\\n                       (lower/imported-unit-names packet {:path name}))),\\n   :module-spec (fn [registry {:keys [name packet strict-types], :as unit} php-address deps]\\n                  (if (class-map? unit)\\n                    (lower/classmap-spec registry unit php-address deps)\\n                    (lower/module-spec registry packet\\n                                       {:path name,\\n                                        :strict-types strict-types,\\n                                        :php-address php-address,\\n                                        :deps deps}))),\\n   :scc-module-specs lower/scc-module-specs,\\n   :site-set siteset/publish!,\\n   :alias-site-set true,\\n   :binder-table nil,\\n   :refusal-ns \\\"yang.php\\\",\\n   :publish-fn publish-fn})\\n\\n\\n(defn publication-order\\n  \\\"Dependency-first names, retaining the input order for acyclic units.\\n   Cyclic components appear as sorted member vectors in :order, one\\n   publication step per SCC. An include edge no unit and no `published`\\n   entry supplies is refused `:yang.php/unpublished-import` by name.\\\"\\n  [units published]\\n  (core/publication-order (config publish/publish-module!) units published))\\n\\n\\n(defn publish-units!\\n  \\\"Publish `units` into the content store `handle` over the host registry\\n   `registry`, `php` pinned at `php-address` and each include outside the\\n   set at its address in `published` ({path address}), in\\n   `publication-order`. Answers `{:status :ok :addresses {path address}\\n   :bundles {bundle-symbol address}}`, or the first refusal:\\n   `publication-order`'s before anything is written, else\\n   `publish-module!`'s with a string `:unit`.\\\"\\n  ([handle registry php-address units]\\n   (publish-units! handle registry php-address units {}))\\n  ([handle registry php-address units published]\\n   (publish-units! handle registry php-address units published publish/publish-module!))\\n  ([handle registry php-address units published publish-fn]\\n   (core/publish-units! (config publish-fn) handle registry php-address units published)))\\nzsh:1: no matches found: src/cljc/yang/*publication*\\n\",\"exit_code\":1,\"status\":\"failed\"}}\ncollab/yang-php/1794569650000-engineer-p7b-ticks-h.gpt-6.1-terra.stdout.log:24:{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_11\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc 'cat src/cljc/yang/python/antlr/siteset.cljc'\",\"aggregated_output\":\"(ns yang.python.antlr.siteset\\n  \\\"C4 slice I7 (docs/design/yang.antlr.md 8.5.6, ruling 14): published site\\n   sets for imported modules.\\n\\n   Site marks never enter canonical rows, so a published module tree\\n   carries none; they live in the frontend-metadata side table. A module's\\n   site set is that side table's `:yang/site` marks for the module tree,\\n   sorted (`yang.safepoint/site-set`) and content-addressed. A separate\\n   publication record, keyed by the module's manifest address, pins the\\n   association: the exact code root (the manifest's `:yin.module/tree`),\\n   the frontend revision, the transformation profile and the set's\\n   address. The module manifest keeps its schema: a manifest address names\\n   code, and a record names a manifest.\\n\\n   Discovery. The publisher signs a publication entry per module,\\n   `{:yang.site/manifest m :yang.site/record record-address ...}`, and\\n   indexes it as the datoms `[e :yang.site/envelope env]` and `[e\\n   :yang.site/proof proof]` beside its name envelopes (`signed-entry`).\\n   A reader `discover`s a manifest's record addresses from the index\\n   datoms it loaded, under the principals it declares; only a declared\\n   principal's signature makes an entry count. The record and the set are\\n   fetched by address and verified against it.\\n\\n   A consumer under a profile requiring hooks `admit`s an import: it\\n   verifies every pin against the published code root and refuses a\\n   missing, empty or mismatched set, naming the profile and the set.\\n   `stage` then applies a verified set at link time, as the linker\\n   composition's `:stage`: it derives the instrumented module `A'` from the\\n   published tree alone, bottom-up over the module's guest requirements,\\n   and publishes it beside its `:derive` record. An explicitly noninterruptible profile imports the module\\n   as published, with none.\\\"\\n  (:require [dao.jing :as jing]\\n            [yang.python.antlr.lower :as lower]\\n            [yang.safepoint :as safepoint]\\n            [yin.vm :as vm]\\n            [yin.vm.encoder :as encoder]\\n            [yin.vm.linker.publish :as publish]\\n            [yin.vm.linker.sign :as sign]))\\n\\n\\n(def frontend-revision\\n  \\\"The frontend revision the marks are pinned to: it moves with any change\\n   to which nodes the Python lowering marks, or how.\\\"\\n  \\\"yang.python.antlr.lower/sites-1\\\")\\n\\n\\n(def lowering-profile\\n  \\\"The transformation profile the marks were made under: the module\\n   emitter over the linked prelude, marking these kinds.\\\"\\n  {:yang.site/unit :module,\\n   :yang.site/prelude :linked,\\n   :yang.site/kinds [:call :line :loop]})\\n\\n\\n(def profile-address\\n  \\\"The address the transformation profile is pinned by.\\\"\\n  (jing/segment-key lowering-profile))\\n\\n\\n(defn- medium\\n  []\\n  :yang.python.antlr/site-set)\\n\\n\\n(defn site-set-of\\n  \\\"`{:root address :tree tree :set [[path mark] ...]}` for the module map\\n   AST `ast`: the address of its canonical tree, the tree's rows and its\\n   sorted site set.\\\"\\n  [ast]\\n  (let [env (encoder/project (encoder/source-envelope\\n                               (medium) [:site-set 0] [ast]))\\n        tree (nth (:yin/batch env) (:yin/root env))\\n        origin (vm/source-origin (:yin/source-medium env)\\n                                 (:yin/batch-token env) (:yin/root env))\\n        sites (safepoint/sites (:yin/frontend-metadata env) origin (:root tree))]\\n    {:root (:root tree), :tree tree, :set (safepoint/site-set sites)}))\\n\\n\\n(defn record\\n  \\\"The publication record associating manifest `manifest` with site set\\n   `set-address` over code root `root`.\\\"\\n  [manifest root set-address]\\n  {:yang.site/manifest manifest,\\n   :yang.site/root root,\\n   :yang.site/revision frontend-revision,\\n   :yang.site/profile profile-address,\\n   :yang.site/set set-address})\\n\\n\\n(defn publish!\\n  \\\"Publish the site set of module `ast` (published at manifest address\\n   `manifest`) into `handle`: the set as one blob, then its record. Answers\\n   `{:record r :record-address a :set-address s :set [...]}`.\\\"\\n  [handle manifest ast]\\n  (let [{:keys [root set]} (site-set-of ast)\\n        set-address (jing/materialize! handle (vec set))\\n        r (record manifest root set-address)]\\n    {:record r,\\n     :record-address (jing/materialize! handle r),\\n     :set-address set-address,\\n     :set set}))\\n\\n\\n(defn- read-at\\n  \\\"The value at `address` in `handle`: `::absent` when the handle holds no\\n   bytes for a segment address (the handle is asked with a not-found\\n   sentinel, as a byte store is), nil when `address` is no segment address\\n   or its bytes do not hash to it, else the value.\\\"\\n  [handle address]\\n  (if-not (jing/segment-address? address)\\n    nil\\n    (let [bs ((:get-bytes-fn handle) address ::absent)]\\n      (cond\\n        (= ::absent bs) ::absent\\n        (nil? bs) ::absent\\n        (jing/segment-bytes-match? address bs) (jing/segment-value address bs)\\n        :else nil))))\\n\\n\\n(defn- absent?\\n  [x]\\n  (= ::absent x))\\n\\n\\n(defn- missing\\n  \\\"The stage's report of the absent `address`: first one met, nothing\\n   written.\\\"\\n  [address]\\n  {:status :missing, :address address})\\n\\n\\n(defn- fetch\\n  \\\"The value at `address` in `handle`, or nil when absent or not its\\n   address.\\\"\\n  [handle address]\\n  (let [v (read-at handle address)]\\n    (when-not (absent? v) v)))\\n\\n\\n;; =============================================================================\\n;; Discovery\\n;; =============================================================================\\n\\n(defn signed-entry\\n  \\\"The publisher `key`'s (`{:seed :public}`) signed publication entry\\n   `{:envelope e :proof p}` for the record at `record-address` of the\\n   module at `manifest`: what the index carries so a reader discovers the\\n   record from the published code.\\\"\\n  [key manifest record-address]\\n  (let [env {:yang.site/op :publish,\\n             :yang.site/manifest manifest,\\n             :yang.site/record record-address,\\n             :yin.module/asserted-by (sign/principal (:public key))}]\\n    {:envelope env, :proof (sign/sign-envelope (:seed key) env)}))\\n\\n\\n(defn entry-datoms\\n  \\\"The index datoms of the signed entries `signed`, entity ids from 0.\\\"\\n  [signed]\\n  (vec (mapcat (fn [e {:keys [envelope proof]}]\\n                 [[e :yang.site/envelope envelope]\\n                  [e :yang.site/proof proof]])\\n               (range) signed)))\\n\\n\\n(defn discover\\n  \\\"The record addresses `datoms` (`[e a v ...]`, a loaded index's) publish\\n   for the module at `manifest`, in datom order: the entries whose proof is\\n   a valid signature by one of the `publics` (64 hex keys) the reader\\n   declares, and no other.\\\"\\n  [datoms publics manifest]\\n  (let [proofs (reduce (fn [acc [e a v]]\\n                         (cond-> acc\\n                           (= a :yang.site/proof) (update e (fnil conj #{}) v)))\\n                       {} datoms)]\\n    (vec (distinct\\n           (keep (fn [[e a env]]\\n                   (when (and (= a :yang.site/envelope)\\n                              (map? env)\\n                              (= :publish (:yang.site/op env))\\n                              (= manifest (:yang.site/manifest env)))\\n                     (let [ps (get proofs e)\\n                           sig (when (= 1 (count ps))\\n                                 (:yin.module/signature (first ps)))]\\n                       (when (and (string? sig)\\n                                  (some #(sign/verify-envelope\\n                                           % (jing/canonical-bytes env) sig)\\n                                        publics))\\n                         (:yang.site/record env)))))\\n                 datoms)))))\\n\\n\\n;; =============================================================================\\n;; Verification and admission\\n;; =============================================================================\\n\\n(defn- refusal\\n  [reason requirement message data]\\n  (merge {:status :refused,\\n          :reason reason,\\n          :profile (:profile requirement),\\n          :message (str \\\"profile \\\" (pr-str (:profile requirement))\\n                        \\\" requires hooks: \\\" message)}\\n         data))\\n\\n\\n(defn- entry-ok?\\n  \\\"A set entry is `[path mark]`, a path vector and a mark of the profile's\\n   kinds (a kind, or `[kind datum ...]`).\\\"\\n  [entry]\\n  (and (vector? entry)\\n       (= 2 (count entry))\\n       (vector? (first entry))\\n       (let [mark (second entry)]\\n         (boolean (some #{(if (vector? mark) (first mark) mark)}\\n                        (:yang.site/kinds lowering-profile))))))\\n\\n\\n(defn verify\\n  \\\"Nil when the record at `record-address` verifies as the site set of the\\n   module published at `manifest` in `handle`; `{:status :missing :address\\n   a}` for the first address (manifest, record, set, in that order) the\\n   handle does not hold; else the first defect `{:defect k ...}` (with the\\n   record's `:set` address when it names one): the manifest or record not\\n   its address, the record naming another manifest, the code root, the\\n   frontend revision or the profile not the published ones, the set not its\\n   address or empty, or an entry not a path with a mark kind of the\\n   profile's.\\\"\\n  [handle manifest record-address]\\n  (let [m (read-at handle manifest)\\n        r (when-not (absent? m) (read-at handle record-address))\\n        set-address (when (map? r) (:yang.site/set r))\\n        set (when-not (or (absent? m) (absent? r))\\n              (read-at handle set-address))\\n        defect (cond\\n                 (not (map? m)) {:defect :manifest-absent}\\n                 (not (map? r)) {:defect :record-absent}\\n                 (not= manifest (:yang.site/manifest r)) {:defect :manifest}\\n                 (not= (:yin.module/tree m) (:yang.site/root r)) {:defect :root}\\n                 (not= frontend-revision (:yang.site/revision r))\\n                 {:defect :revision}\\n                 (not= profile-address (:yang.site/profile r)) {:defect :profile}\\n                 (not (vector? set)) {:defect :set-absent}\\n                 (empty? set) {:defect :set-empty}\\n                 (not (every? entry-ok? set)) {:defect :kind}\\n                 :else nil)]\\n    (cond\\n      (absent? m) (missing manifest)\\n      (absent? r) (missing record-address)\\n      (absent? set) (missing set-address)\\n      defect (cond-> defect set-address (assoc :set set-address)))))\\n\\n\\n(defn admit\\n  \\\"A consumer's decision for the import of the module at `manifest` in\\n   `handle`, `record-addresses` the publication records it discovered for\\n   it, under `requirement`: `{:profile name :hooks true}` or `{:profile\\n   name :noninterruptible true}`. Answers `{:status :ok :instrumented? true\\n   :set [...] :set-address s :record a}` when a record verifies, `{:status\\n   :ok :instrumented? false :note ...}` for a noninterruptible profile,\\n   else a refusal naming the profile and the missing or mismatched set (its\\n   address when the record names one); or `{:status :missing :address a}`,\\n   the first address a record's verification reads that `handle` does not\\n   hold (`verify`).\\\"\\n  [handle manifest record-addresses requirement]\\n  (cond\\n    (:noninterruptible requirement)\\n    {:status :ok,\\n     :instrumented? false,\\n     :note (str \\\"profile \\\" (pr-str (:profile requirement))\\n                \\\" is noninterruptible: imported without a site set\\\")}\\n\\n    (:hooks requirement)\\n    (if (empty? record-addresses)\\n      (refusal :yang.python.antlr/missing-site-set requirement\\n               \\\"no published site set for the module\\\" {:manifest manifest})\\n      (loop [todo record-addresses\\n             first-defect nil]\\n        (if-let [a (first todo)]\\n          (let [v (verify handle manifest a)]\\n            (cond\\n              (nil? v)\\n              (let [set-address (:yang.site/set (fetch handle a))]\\n                {:status :ok,\\n                 :instrumented? true,\\n                 :record a,\\n                 :set-address set-address,\\n                 :set (fetch handle set-address)})\\n\\n              (= :missing (:status v)) v\\n\\n              :else (recur (rest todo) (or first-defect [a v]))))\\n          (let [[a defect] first-defect]\\n            (refusal :yang.python.antlr/site-set-mismatch requirement\\n                     (str \\\"the published site set\\\"\\n                          (when-let [s (:set defect)] (str \\\" \\\" s))\\n                          \\\" does not verify (\\\" (name (:defect defect)) \\\")\\\")\\n                     (assoc defect :manifest manifest :record a))))))\\n\\n    :else\\n    (refusal :yang.python.antlr/profile-undeclared requirement\\n             \\\"the profile declares neither :hooks nor :noninterruptible\\\"\\n             {:manifest manifest})))\\n\\n\\n;; =============================================================================\\n;; Link-time application\\n;; =============================================================================\\n\\n(defn- load-tree\\n  \\\"The canonical row set `{:root :rows}` of the tree at `root` in `handle`,\\n   every row fetched by its address, depth-first: `{:status :missing\\n   :address a}` for the first row the handle does not hold, nil when a row\\n   is not its address or no tree row.\\\"\\n  [handle root]\\n  (loop [todo [root]\\n         rows {}]\\n    (if-let [id (peek todo)]\\n      (if (contains? rows id)\\n        (recur (pop todo) rows)\\n        (let [body (read-at handle id)\\n              slots (when (vector? body)\\n                      (get vm/semantic-bytecode-grammar (first body)))]\\n          (cond\\n            (absent? body) (missing id)\\n            slots (recur (into (pop todo)\\n                               (mapcat (fn [[_ kind] v]\\n                                         (case kind\\n                                           :node [v]\\n                                           :nodes v\\n                                           nil))\\n                                       slots\\n                                       (rest body)))\\n                         (assoc rows id (into [id] body)))\\n            :else nil)))\\n      {:root root, :rows rows})))\\n\\n\\n(defn- derive-module\\n  \\\"Publish into `handle` the instrumented module `A'` of the module `m` at\\n   `manifest` for the verified set of `adm`: the published tree with the\\n   hooks of `(:hooks-profile requirement)` at its sites, requiring\\n   `(:pysp requirement)` beside its own requirements, each guest\\n   requirement rebound through `derived` ({A A'}). The `:derive` record\\n   (`yang.safepoint/derive-record`) is materialized beside it and its\\n   address answered as `:derivation`; nothing transacts it into a ledger.\\n   Answers `{:status :ok :address a' :derivation d}`, a refusal, or\\n   `{:status :missing :address a}` for the first tree row the handle does\\n   not hold, before anything is written.\\\"\\n  [handle registry manifest m adm requirement derived]\\n  (let [fail (fn [message data]\\n               (refusal :yang.python.antlr/instrumentation-failed requirement\\n                        message (assoc data :manifest manifest)))]\\n    (if-not (jing/segment-address? (:pysp requirement))\\n      (fail \\\"the hook module is not published\\\" {})\\n      (try\\n        (let [tree (load-tree handle (:yin.module/tree m))\\n              absent (= :missing (:status tree))\\n              sites (into {} (:set adm))\\n              inserted (when (and tree (not absent))\\n                         (safepoint/insert tree sites\\n                                           (:hooks-profile requirement)))\\n              ast (when inserted\\n                    (vm/semantic-bytecode->ast (:tree inserted)))\\n              runtime (or (:runtime requirement) '#{py pysp yang.call})\\n              out (when ast\\n                    (publish/publish-module!\\n                      handle\\n                      {:name (:yin.module/name m),\\n                       :ast ast,\\n                       :exports (:yin.module/exports m),\\n                       :requires (assoc (into {}\\n                                              (map (fn [[n a]]\\n                                                     [n (if (contains? runtime n)\\n                                                          a\\n                                                          (get derived a a))]))\\n                                              (:yin.module/requires m))\\n                                        'pysp (:pysp requirement)),\\n                       :primitives (into {}\\n                                         (map (fn [[n _]]\\n                                                [n (lower/primitive-declaration\\n                                                     registry n)]))\\n                                         (:yin.module/primitives m))}))]\\n          (cond\\n            absent tree\\n            (nil? tree) (fail \\\"the module's tree is not published\\\" {})\\n            (= :refused (:status out))\\n            (fail \\\"the instrumented module did not publish\\\" {:cause out})\\n            :else\\n            {:status :ok,\\n             :address (:address out),\\n             :instrumented? true,\\n             :set-address (:set-address adm),\\n             :derivation (jing/materialize!\\n                           handle\\n                           (safepoint/derive-record\\n                             (:yin.module/tree m)\\n                             (:yin.module/tree (:manifest out))\\n                             (:hooks-profile requirement)\\n                             (:sites inserted)))}))\\n        (catch #?(:cljd Object :clj Exception :cljs :default) e\\n          (fail (str \\\"the instrumented module did not derive: \\\" (ex-message e))\\n                {}))))))\\n\\n\\n(defn- records-for\\n  \\\"The record addresses for `manifest`: the local map `records`'s, then\\n   those `ctx`'s loaded index datoms publish under its principals.\\\"\\n  [ctx records manifest]\\n  (into (vec (get records manifest))\\n        (discover (:datoms ctx) (:principals ctx) manifest)))\\n\\n\\n(defn- derive-closure\\n  \\\"The instrumented module of `manifest` under a hook profile, derived\\n   bottom-up: each guest requirement is admitted and derived first, so the\\n   derived module pins the derived requirements it will link (`derived`,\\n   {A A'}, derives a shared requirement once). Answers `{:status :ok\\n   :address a' :derivation d :derived derived}`, the first refusal, with\\n   `:name` the requirement that was refused and `:via` the module that\\n   required it, or `{:status :missing :address a}` for the first address\\n   (record, set, guest manifest, tree row) read in a fixed depth-first\\n   order that the handle does not hold. Nothing is written until every\\n   read has succeeded.\\\"\\n  [ctx registry records requirement manifest derived]\\n  (let [handle (:store ctx)\\n        runtime (or (:runtime requirement) '#{py pysp yang.call})\\n        adm (admit handle manifest (records-for ctx records manifest)\\n                   requirement)]\\n    (if (not= :ok (:status adm))\\n      adm\\n      (let [m (fetch handle manifest)\\n            deps (sort-by (comp str key)\\n                          (remove (comp runtime key)\\n                                  (:yin.module/requires m)))\\n            below (reduce (fn [acc [n a]]\\n                            (if (contains? (:derived acc) a)\\n                              acc\\n                              (let [r (derive-closure ctx registry records\\n                                                      requirement a\\n                                                      (:derived acc))]\\n                                (case (:status r)\\n                                  :ok (assoc acc :derived (:derived r))\\n                                  :missing (reduced r)\\n                                  (reduced (cond-> (assoc r :via manifest)\\n                                             (nil? (:name r)) (assoc :name n)))))))\\n                          {:derived derived}\\n                          deps)]\\n        (if (:status below)\\n          below\\n          (let [out (derive-module handle registry manifest m adm requirement\\n                                   (:derived below))]\\n            (if (not= :ok (:status out))\\n              out\\n              (assoc out :derived (assoc (:derived below) manifest\\n                                         (:address out))))))))))\\n\\n\\n(defn- overlay\\n  \\\"A write-buffering handle over `base`: reads see the buffer first, then\\n   `base`; puts land in the buffer only, in put order. `flush!` writes the\\n   buffer to `base`; dropping the overlay writes nothing.\\\"\\n  [base]\\n  (let [buf (atom {:bytes {}, :order []})\\n        sentinel #?(:cljs (js-obj)\\n                    :default (Object.))]\\n    {:handle (assoc base\\n                    :get-bytes-fn (fn [address not-found]\\n                                    (let [bs (get-in @buf [:bytes address])]\\n                                      (if (nil? bs)\\n                                        ((:get-bytes-fn base) address not-found)\\n                                        bs)))\\n                    :put-bytes-fn (fn [address bs]\\n                                    (if (or (contains? (:bytes @buf) address)\\n                                            (let [existing ((:get-bytes-fn base)\\n                                                            address\\n                                                            sentinel)]\\n                                              (and (some? existing)\\n                                                   (not (identical? sentinel\\n                                                                    existing)))))\\n                                      :present\\n                                      (do (swap! buf (fn [b]\\n                                                       (-> b\\n                                                           (assoc-in [:bytes address]\\n                                                                     bs)\\n                                                           (update :order conj\\n                                                                   address))))\\n                                          :inserted))))\\n     :flush! (fn []\\n               (let [{:keys [bytes order]} @buf]\\n                 (doseq [a order] ((:put-bytes-fn base) a (get bytes a)))))}))\\n\\n\\n(def reasons\\n  \\\"The refusal reasons the stage answers, the composition's own.\\\"\\n  #{:yang.python.antlr/missing-site-set\\n    :yang.python.antlr/site-set-mismatch\\n    :yang.python.antlr/profile-undeclared\\n    :yang.python.antlr/instrumentation-failed})\\n\\n\\n(defn stage\\n  \\\"The link-time stage (`yin.vm.linker.interpreter/composition`'s `:stage`)\\n   of a Python import under `requirement`: `{:profile name :hooks true\\n   :hooks-profile p :pysp address}` or `{:profile name :noninterruptible\\n   true}`, with `:runtime` the names of the runtime modules (default `py`,\\n   `pysp`, and `yang.call`) it passes through. `yang.call` is the shared\\n   call dispatcher, not guest Python code, so it has no Python site marks.\\n   `records` ({manifest [record-address ...]})\\n   are the local publication's records; a DHT source supplies none and the\\n   stage discovers them from the index datoms of its context. The stage\\n   reads only the context's `:store` and never fetches: it answers\\n   `{:status :missing :address a}` for the first address that store does\\n   not hold, and is run again once it is there. Answers\\n   `(fn [ctx name address])`: `{:status :ok :address address}` as published\\n   when noninterruptible, else the address of the instrumented module\\n   derived bottom-up from the verified sets (`:derivation` its `:derive`\\n   record's address), or the refusal naming the profile, the set and the\\n   `:name` refused.\\\"\\n  [registry requirement records]\\n  (let [runtime (or (:runtime requirement) '#{py pysp yang.call})]\\n    (fn [ctx name address]\\n      (cond\\n        (contains? runtime name) {:status :ok, :address address}\\n\\n        (:noninterruptible requirement)\\n        (assoc (admit (:store ctx) address [] requirement) :address address)\\n\\n        :else\\n        (let [{:keys [handle flush!]} (overlay (:store ctx))\\n              r (derive-closure (assoc ctx :store handle) registry records\\n                                requirement address {})]\\n          (case (:status r)\\n            :ok (do (flush!)\\n                    (select-keys r [:status :address :derivation :instrumented?\\n                                    :set-address]))\\n            :missing (select-keys r [:status :address])\\n            (cond-> r (nil? (:name r)) (assoc :name name))))))))\\n\\n\\n(defn signed-entries\\n  \\\"The `publisher` key's signed publication entries for the site sets\\n   `publish-units!` answered: `sites` (`:sites`), the manifest of each\\n   from `addresses` (a Python name) or `bundles` (a bundle symbol), in\\n   key order. The publisher that asserts these names indexes them in the\\n   same transaction (`entry-datoms`); a module with no entry is refused\\n   by every hook-profile reader.\\\"\\n  [publisher sites addresses bundles]\\n  (mapv (fn [[k s]]\\n          (signed-entry publisher (or (get addresses k) (get bundles k))\\n                        (:record-address s)))\\n        (sort-by (comp str key) sites)))\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}\ncollab/yang-php/1794569650000-engineer-p7b-ticks-h.gpt-6.1-terra.stdout.log:33:{\"type\":\"item.started\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_16\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"python3 - <<'PY'\\np='src/cljc/yang/php/antlr/siteset.cljc'\\ns=open(p).read().replace('[yin.vm.encoder :as encoder]))','[yin.vm.encoder :as encoder]\\\\\\\\n    [yin.vm.linker.sign :as sign]))')\\ns += '''\\n\\n(defn signed-entry\\n  \\\\\\\"Sign the association between a published manifest and its site record.\\\\\\\"\\n  [key manifest record-address]\\n  (let [env {:yang.site/op :publish,\\n             :yang.site/manifest manifest,\\n             :yang.site/record record-address,\\n             :yin.module/asserted-by (sign/principal (:public key))}]\\n    {:envelope env, :proof (sign/sign-envelope (:seed key) env)}))\\n\\n\\n(defn entry-datoms\\n  \\\\\\\"Index signed site publications alongside name assertions.\\\\\\\"\\n  [signed]\\n  (vec (mapcat (fn [e {:keys [envelope proof]}]\\n                 [[e :yang.site/envelope envelope]\\n                  [e :yang.site/proof proof]])\\n               (range) signed)))\\n\\n\\n(defn discover\\n  \\\\\\\"Find site record addresses signed by a declared public key for manifest.\\\\\\\"\\n  [datoms publics manifest]\\n  (let [proofs (reduce (fn [acc [e a v]]\\n                         (cond-> acc\\n                           (= a :yang.site/proof) (update e (fnil conj #{}) v)))\\n                       {} datoms)]\\n    (vec (distinct\\n           (keep (fn [[e a env]]\\n                   (when (and (= a :yang.site/envelope) (map? env)\\n                              (= :publish (:yang.site/op env))\\n                              (= manifest (:yang.site/manifest env)))\\n                     (let [ps (get proofs e)\\n                           sig (when (= 1 (count ps))\\n                                 (:yin.module/signature (first ps)))]\\n                       (when (and (string? sig)\\n                                  (some #(and (= (:yin.module/asserted-by env)\\n                                                 (sign/principal %))\\n                                              (sign/verify-envelope\\n                                                % (jing/canonical-bytes env) sig))\\n                                        publics))\\n                         (:yang.site/record env)))))\\n                 datoms)))))\\n\\n\\n(defn signed-entries\\n  \\\\\\\"Sign all site publications returned by unit publication, including aliases.\\\\\\\"\\n  [publisher sites addresses bundles]\\n  (mapv (fn [[k s]]\\n          (signed-entry publisher (or (get addresses k) (get bundles k))\\n                        (:record-address s)))\\n        (sort-by (comp str key) sites)))\\n'''\\nopen(p,'w').write(s)\\np='test/yang/php/antlr/siteset_test.clj';s=open(p).read().replace('[yang.php.antlr.siteset :as siteset]))','[yang.php.antlr.siteset :as siteset]\\\\\\\\n    [yin.vm.linker.sign :as sign]))');s+='''\\n\\n(deftest signed-discovery-requires-an-authorized-untampered-entry\\n  (let [key (sign/generate)\\n        other (sign/generate)\\n        entry (siteset/signed-entry key :manifest :record)\\n        datoms (siteset/entry-datoms [entry])\\n        tampered (siteset/entry-datoms [(assoc-in entry [:envelope :yang.site/record] :other)])]\\n    (is (= [:record] (siteset/discover datoms [(:public key)] :manifest)))\\n    (is (empty? (siteset/discover datoms [(:public other)] :manifest)))\\n    (is (empty? (siteset/discover datoms [(:public key)] :other)))\\n    (is (empty? (siteset/discover tampered [(:public key)] :manifest)))))\\n''';open(p,'w').write(s)\\nPY\\nclojure -M:test -n yang.php.antlr.siteset-test\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"\",\"exit_code\":null,\"status\":\"in_progress\"}}\ncollab/yang-php/1794569650000-engineer-p7b-ticks-h.gpt-6.1-terra.stdout.log:34:{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_16\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"python3 - <<'PY'\\np='src/cljc/yang/php/antlr/siteset.cljc'\\ns=open(p).read().replace('[yin.vm.encoder :as encoder]))','[yin.vm.encoder :as encoder]\\\\\\\\n    [yin.vm.linker.sign :as sign]))')\\ns += '''\\n\\n(defn signed-entry\\n  \\\\\\\"Sign the association between a published manifest and its site record.\\\\\\\"\\n  [key manifest record-address]\\n  (let [env {:yang.site/op :publish,\\n             :yang.site/manifest manifest,\\n             :yang.site/record record-address,\\n             :yin.module/asserted-by (sign/principal (:public key))}]\\n    {:envelope env, :proof (sign/sign-envelope (:seed key) env)}))\\n\\n\\n(defn entry-datoms\\n  \\\\\\\"Index signed site publications alongside name assertions.\\\\\\\"\\n  [signed]\\n  (vec (mapcat (fn [e {:keys [envelope proof]}]\\n                 [[e :yang.site/envelope envelope]\\n                  [e :yang.site/proof proof]])\\n               (range) signed)))\\n\\n\\n(defn discover\\n  \\\\\\\"F